title: Choosing keybindings
tags: Keybindings
kind: rationale
summary: Why the keys are where they are: characters typed alike on bépo, AZERTY and QWERTY, single letters for the constant few, a domain then a verb for the rest, and the shortcuts every program has.


The shipped keys were laid out again in October 2026, once the commands were
many, on rules meant to last. They are written here, and the ones a program can
check are checked on the shipped file (`test_shipped_keys_follow_the_rules_of_choice`).
The keys themselves are in [[Keyboard navigation]].

!! What can be typed

A key is matched by the ''character'' it types, not by where it is on the keyboard
([[Keybindings]]). The shipped keys must mean the same on bépo, AZERTY and QWERTY,
so they are made of:

* ''letters'' — the keys most at hand, and the most precious;
* `,` `-` `=`, which the three layouts type without a modifier;
* the ''named keys'': arrows, Enter, Delete, F1…F12, Home, PageUp…;
* digits and `. / ; : ! * ( ) " $ % ?` ''alone'' only: each needs Shift on one of the layouts, so it counts as a modified key, and under Ctrl or Alt the Shift that layout needs changes the character — `ctrl+.` cannot be typed on AZERTY.

Never a key that needs AltGr (`[ ] { } \ | ~ ^ # @ & _ < >`), a dead key, or an
accented letter.

What each of these keys costs gives it a ''level'', from 0 — a letter alone — to 3 — a
Ctrl or Alt chord with Shift; [[Key levels]] lists them, with those used and left.

!! How often, how many keys

Every command falls in a class, by how often it is used — written in
`gui/command-classes.toml` ([[Command classes]]):

|!Class |!Used |!Gets |
|A |all the time |one key |
|B |several times a session |one modifier, or two keys |
|C |now and then |two or three keys |
|D |rarely |the command input only |
|✕ |cannot be undone |never a key |

A ''single letter'' is spent only on class A: `x` the command input, `o` the other
panel, `f` find, Space. Every other letter starts a ''domain''.

!! A domain, then a verb

The first key names a domain — what the command is about — and the second a verb,
from one table: `a` add, `s` set, `r` remove a value, `u` unset, `e` edit, `n`
rename, `t` retype, `d` delete, `c` create, `q` query, `o` open, `l` list, `p` run,
`z` revert, `k` keep, `i` index. Shift is a variant of the same verb: `p e` edits a
field, `p E` edits it from empty. The position gives a letter its role: `t` is the
panel types first, retype second.

There are no verbs alone in first position: `d` alone deleting would spend a letter
on what `m d` and `e d` say as well, and leave fewer for the domains.

* Domains of nouns: `t` panel types, `g` go to, `v` view, `h` help, `w` workspaces, `c` the clipboard.
* Domains of verbs: `m` the selected metarecord (Shift+m or `m m` the checked ones, `m q` the query's), `e` the items, `,` the tags, `p` the focused panel's own actions.

The letters with a role are variables of `keybindings.toml`, so moving a domain or
a verb is one line ([[Keybinding variables]]).

!! Where it opens

A command that opens something takes a target: `i` here, `b` beside (the other
slot), `n` a new workspace, typed before its key. Without one, an action started
from a row of the panel goes beside, so that the row stays in view; one picked
from a list — a recent metarecord, a folder — goes here, in place of the list.

!! The keys of every program

What every program does has the key it has everywhere: Ctrl+c, Ctrl+z, Ctrl+f,
Ctrl+q quits, Ctrl+r and F5 read again, F2 renames, F11 is full screen, F12 the
developer tools, Ctrl+Tab the next tab. The rules add an alias where the common key
breaks them — `:` for the command input needs Shift on bépo and AZERTY, hence `x`.
Two conventions are left out on purpose: `j`/`k` (the arrows do it, and they would
cost two letters) and `n`/`p` for next and previous.

Tab moves between the elements of a panel, as in a browser; it never changes panel
— `o` and F6 do.

!! What costs more

A key that destroys costs more than one that does not: Delete sends to the trash,
which gives back; nothing that cannot be undone has a key. Shift+Delete deletes
the metarecord alone, the file kept — a narrower form, under the same key.
